l":"Claude"},{"repo_name":"andremichelle/openDAW","commit_timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:00:31Z","branch":"main","commit_sha":"b462be8f8596026426f49c89c67425f322afb71c","github_link":"https://github.com/andremichelle/openDAW/commit/b462be8f8596026426f49c89c67425f322afb71c","commit_subject":"WebCLAP plugins keep their samples in the project (opendaw.samples/1). A WclapSampleBox under a WebCLAP device's new samples field (key 16 on all three boxes) links a key the plugin chose to an AudioFileBox, so saves, undo and live rooms carry only the link and the audio stays in the sample storage. The three WebCLAP devices expose the hub like Apparat (observe_sample_collection_field, sample_changed), the engine loads and delivers the samples the same way, and the bridge copies a sample's frames into the plugin once they are resident (prepare, commit, clear on the plugin's opendaw.samples/1) and tells the plugin through the host's that the host keeps them. A plugin page hands the studio a file for a key (webclapSample, relayed by wclap-frame.html): the studio imports it, or links the sample it was dragged from in the browser, and points the slot at it; an emptied slot keeps its box. The engine re-binds a device's sample slots with its parameters, so a slot added while the plugin runs is delivered (Apparat rebuilt the device on every change, a WebCLAP device does not). test/wclap-samples.test.ts runs Radar (open-webclap, MIT, AGPL-3.0 as built) through the engine, test/assets/xenon.wasm is Xenon with the extension.","ai_tool":"Claude"},{"repo_name":"apache/camel","commit_timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:00:28Z","branch":"main","commit_sha":"b240602dbb1d91a1cfe5d454679b68a76fb6e06b","github_link":"https://github.com/apache/camel/commit/b240602dbb1d91a1cfe5d454679b68a76fb6e06b","commit_subject":"CAMEL-26441: camel-launcher - The distribution runs the Camel CLI from the Spring Boot fat jar, which is slower and has no class data sharing (#28776)","ai_tool":"Claude"},{"repo_name":"apache/camel","